<quiz>
  <question type="stack">
    <name>
      <text>Polynomials: differentiate term by term (INNODEMS)</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>The <strong>sum rule</strong> and the <strong>constant multiple rule</strong> let us differentiate a polynomial one term at a time: for each term \( c\,x^{n} \), multiply the coefficient by the power and reduce the power by one,
\[ \frac{d}{dx}\left(c\,x^{n}\right) = n\,c\,x^{\,n-1}, \qquad \frac{d}{dx}(\text{constant}) = 0. \]</p>
<p>Find \( f'(x) \) for</p>
\[ f(x) = {@fx@}. \]
<div>\( f'(x) = \) [[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]]</div>
<div>[[feedback:prt1]]</div>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<h3>Worked Solution</h3>
<p>Differentiate each term separately (sum rule), keeping each coefficient as a multiplier (constant multiple rule), and apply the power rule \( \frac{d}{dx}(x^n) = n x^{n-1} \) to each power:</p>
\[
\begin{align*}
f'(x) &= \frac{d}{dx}\left({@ca4*x^4@}\right) + \frac{d}{dx}\left({@ca3*x^3@}\right) + \frac{d}{dx}\left({@ca2*x^2@}\right) + \frac{d}{dx}\left({@ca1*x@}\right) + \frac{d}{dx}\left({@ca0@}\right) \\
&= 4\cdot({@ca4@})\,x^{3} + 3\cdot({@ca3@})\,x^{2} + 2\cdot({@ca2@})\,x + ({@ca1@}) + 0 \\
&= {@dfx@}.
\end{align*}
\]
<p>Notice that the constant term \( {@ca0@} \) disappears: a constant does not change, so its rate of change is \( 0 \). The linear term \( {@ca1*x@} \) becomes the constant \( {@ca1@} \), because \( x = x^{1} \) differentiates to \( 1\cdot x^{0} = 1 \).</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
